{=============================================================================
  Air-standard Otto cycle
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0049
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Theoretical (air-standard) Otto cycle solved as in the original exercise:
  an ideal gas with a variable specific heat, the ratio of the specific heats
  gamma being evaluated at the mean temperature of each of the two isentropic
  transformations, and c_v taken as constant over each transformation. The
  states 1 (start of compression), 2 (end of compression), 3 (end of the
  constant-volume heat addition) and 4 (end of expansion) are given by the
  ideal-gas law, the Laplace law p*v^gamma = constant on 1-2 and 3-4 and
  isochoric transformations on 2-3 and 4-1.

  Inputs : r_v, q, p[1], T[1] (cycle); V_d, n (engine)
  Outputs: p_max, T_max, w_net_cycle, eta_otto, W_net, W_dot_net
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiège, course "Thermodynamique appliquée" (MECA0002), session R5,
           exercise 1 (EES file R05_E01_2022.EES) - see README.md
 =============================================================================}

"Inputs"
fluid$ = 'Air'                 "working fluid: air, treated as an ideal gas (as in the original)"
q = 800E3 [J/kg]               "heat added per unit of air in the constant-volume heating 2-3 (800 kJ/kg in the original)"
r = 287 [J/(kg-K)]             "specific gas constant of air (0.287 kJ/(kg-K) in the original); kept for reference only, no equation of the original uses it - they use r_bis"
r_bis = R#/molarmass(fluid$)   "specific gas constant [J/(kg-K)]: universal gas constant divided by the molar mass (as in the original, which used molarmass(Air_ha))"
r_v = 8                        "compression ratio, which is in fact the volumetric ratio v_1/v_2 [-]"
p[1] = 100E3 [Pa]              "pressure at the beginning of the compression (100 kPa in the original)"
T[1] = 17 [C]                  "temperature at the beginning of the compression (17 °C, written 17 [K]+273.15 [K] in the original)"
V_d = 1.6E-3 [m^3]             "total displacement of the engine"
n = 4000 [rev/min]             "engine speed (4-stroke: one cycle per two revolutions)"

"Here the whole cycle is solved with air as an ideal gas, as in the original"

"!Solution"

"Isentropic compression 1-2 : p*v^gamma = Cst ==> Laplace law"
gamma01 = cp(fluid$, T=(T[1]+T[2])/2)/cv(fluid$, T=(T[1]+T[2])/2)  "cp and cv are approximated by evaluating them at the intermediate conditions"
"T2 is not known: the problem is recursive. It is nevertheless solved simultaneously; had this recursive problem not been solvable, a first approximation would have been to use the inlet conditions T[1] only"

v[1] = r_bis*(T[1]+273.15)/p[1]      "specific volume at state 1 from the ideal-gas law (the EES 'volume' function could have been used)"

p[2]*v[2]^gamma01 = p[1]*v[1]^gamma01  "Laplace law"

v[2] = v[1]/r_v               "specific volume at state 2 from the volumetric ratio"

p[2] = r_bis*(T[2]+273.15)/v[2]      "pressure at state 2 from the ideal-gas law"

w_12 + q_12 = cv12*(T[2]-T[1])  "first law for a closed system, giving the heat exchanged: q+w = du"
q_12 = 0                      "isentropic evolution = reversible adiabatic (valid except in some rare steady-state cases); here the transformation is fast, no time for heat exchange = adiabatic"

cv12 = cv(fluid$, T=(T[1]+T[2])/2)  "c_v is assumed constant over the transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations"


"Constant-volume heating 2-3 : v = Cst"

v[3] = v[2]                   "isochoric evolution"
w_23 = 0                      "no work exchanged, isochoric: -integral(p dv) = 0"
q_23 = q                      "heat added, given by the statement"
w_23 + q_23 = cv23*(T[3]-T[2])  "heat exchanged, first law for a closed system"
p[3] = r_bis*(T[3]+273.15)/v[3]      "p_3 from the ideal-gas law"
cv23 = cv(fluid$, T=(T[2]+T[3])/2)  "c_v is assumed constant over the transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations"
T_max = T[3]                  "the maximum pressures and temperatures of the Otto cycle are reached after the heating"
p_max = p[3]

"Isentropic expansion 3-4 : p*v^gamma = Cst ==> Laplace law"
gamma34 = cp(fluid$, T=(T[3]+T[4])/2)/cv(fluid$, T=(T[3]+T[4])/2)  "cp and cv are approximated by evaluating them at the intermediate conditions"
"T4 is not known: the problem is recursive. It is nevertheless solved simultaneously; had this recursive problem not been solvable, a first approximation would have been to use the inlet conditions T[3] only"


v[4] = v[1]                   "the volume is known at state 4 (see below), since the following transformation is isochoric"
p[4] = r_bis*(T[4]+273.15)/v[4]      "p_4 from the ideal-gas law, the specific volume at state 4 being known"

p[3]*v[3]^gamma34 = p[4]*v[4]^gamma34  "Laplace law"



w_34 + q_34 = cv34*(T[4]-T[3])  "heat received during the expansion, first law for a closed system"
q_34 = 0                      "isentropic evolution = reversible adiabatic (valid except in some rare steady-state cases); here the transformation is fast, no time for heat exchange = adiabatic"

cv34 = cv(fluid$, T=(T[3]+T[4])/2)  "c_v is assumed constant over the transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations"


"Constant-volume cooling 4-1 : v = Cst"
w_41 = 0                      "no work exchanged, isochoric"

w_41 + q_41 = cv41*(T[1]-T[4])  "heat exchanged, first law for a closed system"
cv41 = cv(fluid$, T=(T[1]+T[4])/2)  "c_v is assumed constant over the transformation, which would not be judicious for large temperature variations"

w_net_cycle = w_12+w_23+w_34+w_41  "sum of the work of all the steps; they already carry the correct signs (what is supplied to the system is negative)"
w_net_cycle_bis = -(q_23+q_41)  "the work of the ideal cycle can also be seen as the difference between the heat added and the heat rejected; by conservation of energy, what is not rejected as heat is converted into work"
"the two values are not exactly equal because the coefficients gamma, cp and cv are treated as constants"

eta_otto = abs(w_net_cycle/q)

"Mass of air admitted in the cylinders at each intake"
m = V_d/v[1]

W_net = m*w_net_cycle        "work done per cycle [J]; as in the original, the work is negative when it is delivered by the system"

W_dot_net = W_net*n/2/60   "the swept volume of the cylinders is covered twice per Otto cycle: 2 revolutions of the engine (n in rev/min, 60 s/min) for one cycle, work in J -> power in W"


"For information"
s[1] = entropy('Air', T=T[1], P=p[1])
s[2] = entropy('Air', T=T[2], P=p[2])
s[3] = entropy('Air', T=T[3], P=p[3])
s[4] = entropy('Air', T=T[4], P=p[4])